!<
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Space Hunter Lazy Workflow -- shared content.
% Included by SHLazyWorkflowPart1.pidoc and SHLazyWorkflowPart2.pidoc inside
% their "Requirements and License" section. Do not compile on its own (the
% "!<" line above makes the compiler skip it if you do).
% Checked against Part 1 v2.5 / Part 2 v2.3 TEST, 2026-10-11.
% "Files, Programs and Internet Access" checked against Part 1 v2.5.11 /
% Part 2 v2.3.8, 2026-10-23 (CPD program rules: document external programs,
% network connections and file locations). ASTAP removed for Part 1 v2.6.0,
% 2026-10-30 (CPD rule 5.8): blind solving by ImageSolver 6.6.0.
% Checked against Part 1 v2.7.6 / Part 2 v2.4.6, 2026-11-02 (release review):
% ImageSolver's online catalogs, MARS folder, no SXT->StarNet2 fallback.
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\subsection { \label requirements Requirements } {

   \s {Both parts}

   \list[spaced] {
      { Windows operating system. }
      { PixInsight 1.9.6 or later. }
      { An image from a \s {one-shot colour (OSC)} camera, captured with a
        broadband, light-pollution or single dual-narrowband (DNB) filter.
        The RGB channels are \e not split into separate greyscale images. }
      { An image that is already cropped. If you stack in WBPP with
        \e {Auto Crop} and \e {Astrometric Solution} enabled, you can start
        without cropping and the plate solve step is skipped. }
   }

   \s {Part 1}

   \list[spaced] {
      { The MARS database (any drive). }
      { For images without coordinates or image scale in their FITS
        keywords: the Gaia astrometric index file
        (\c {gdr3-idx-1.0.0-g17.xpsd}, about 1.8 GB, from the PixInsight
        Software Distribution), set as \e {Astrometric Index File} in
        \m {Process > Astrometry > Gaia} preferences. ImageSolver's blind
        solver needs it. }
      { Recommended: the local Gaia DR3 star catalogue (XPSD files), set in
        the same Gaia preferences. Without it, ImageSolver uses an online
        catalogue (see \lref access {Files, Programs and Internet Access}). }
   }

   \s {Part 2}

   \list[spaced] {
      { \xref {https://www.rc-astro.com/} {BlurXTerminator, StarXTerminator
        and NoiseXTerminator}. }
      { Optional: \xref {https://www.starnetastro.com/download/} {StarNet2},
        installed from its PixInsight repository. }
      { Optional: MLDenoise and its model file (\c .xmlm), saved on this
        computer. They are available only to licensed PixInsight users: log
        in on \xref {https://pixinsight.com/} {pixinsight.com} with your
        license to download them. }
      { AutoColor is fully integrated into Part 2. You don't need to install
        or load the AutoColor script separately. (The stand-alone AutoColor
        V8 script is in the
        \xref {https://mopscripts.github.io/mop-v8-scripts/} {MOP V8 scripts
        repository}, if you want to use it on its own.) }
      { Internet access only if you use \e {Check dust map} in the background
        pre-check. }
   }

   \note {
      If Part 1 can't find the MARS database, it asks you for its folder.
      That folder is saved and searched first on later runs; \e {Reset
      Settings} forgets it. If a tool that Part 2 calls (BlurXTerminator,
      StarXTerminator, StarNet2, NoiseXTerminator, MLDenoise) isn't
      installed, that step is skipped with a warning in the console. Select
      StarNet2 instead of StarXTerminator yourself if you use it.
   }

   If you combine two DNB filters (Ha/OIII and SII/OIII) into a Hubble-palette
   image, these scripts are not the right tool.
}

\subsection { \label access Files, Programs and Internet Access } {

   Besides your open images, this is everything the scripts store, write,
   start or download.

   \list[spaced] {
      { \s {Settings:} workflow and equipment settings, and the MARS
        folder you picked, are stored in PixInsight's own settings
        (\e {Reset Settings} in Part 1 clears them). }
      { \s {Re-run marks:} each step leaves a small mark (an XISF property)
        on the image, so a second run can skip steps that are still
        applied. The marks are saved only in XISF files. }
      { \s {Part 1, ImageSolver:} uses PixInsight's own ImageSolver, which
        writes temporary star lists (\c {stars*.csv}) to the system temporary
        folder. Its blind solver reads the astrometric index file set in
        the Gaia preferences. The scripts start no external programs. }
      { \s {Part 1, ImageSolver star catalogue (internet):} ImageSolver
        uses the local Gaia star catalogue (XPSD) when it is installed. If
        it isn't, or for very bright limit magnitudes (5 or less), ImageSolver
        downloads the stars it needs from an online catalogue (Bright Stars,
        TYCHO-2 or Gaia DR2) through the VizieR service set in ImageSolver's
        preferences. It sends the image's centre coordinates and search
        radius. This is ImageSolver's own behaviour; install the local Gaia
        catalogue to solve offline. }
      { \s {Part 1, MARS:} the script looks for \c .xmars files in the
        PixInsight folders and in common \c Astro folders, reading only. }
      { \s {Part 1, separation profiles:} profiles are \c .txt files in the
        folder you choose. They are only read for their two factors, never
        run, and written only when you save one. }
      { \s {Part 2, Check dust map:} only when you click it, Part 2 sends the
        image's centre coordinates and field size to the NASA/IPAC IRSA dust
        service over https and downloads the dust map from that service only
        to the system temporary folder. The files are deleted afterwards,
        also when a download fails. No other data is sent. If the first
        attempt fails, the script tries once more. Apart from this and
        ImageSolver's online catalogue (above), the scripts don't use the
        internet. }
   }
}

\subsection { \label buttons The Button Bar } {

   \center {
      \image[width:480px] images/Part1_3.png
   }

   The buttons at the bottom of the dialog, from left to right:

   \definition {
      { New Instance }     { Drag the triangle onto the workspace to save
                             the current settings as a process icon. }
      { User Guide }       { Opens the user guide. }
      { Deselect All }     { Clears every workflow step. }
      { Reset Workflow }   { Returns the workflow steps to their defaults.
                             Equipment settings are not changed. }
      { Reset Settings }   { (gear) Returns the equipment settings (camera,
                             filters, white reference, gradient scales) to
                             their defaults. Workflow steps are not changed. }
   }

   Workflow and equipment settings are saved as you change them in the
   dialog, so they are kept even if you click \s {Cancel} (for example to
   look something up first), and come back the next time you open the
   script. \s {Reset Settings} clears the saved equipment settings at once.
}

